## Quick Reference Checklist

| Area | Checklist Item | Why it matters |
|------|-----------------|----------------|
| **Defining** | `@Retention` present & correct | Determines if the annotation exists at compile, class, or runtime. |
| | `@Target` covers all intended elements | Prevents accidental misuse. |
| | `@Documented` when you want it in Javadoc | Keeps API docs accurate. |
| | `@Inherited` only if needed | Avoid surprising inheritance. |
| | `@Repeatable` for multi‑annotation support | Avoid duplicate annotation errors. |
| | Defaults are compile‑time constants | Annotation is legal. |
| | No mutable state | Annotation instances are effectively constants. |
| **Using** | Import the correct annotation | Avoid semantic drift. |
| | Annotation present at runtime when needed | Otherwise reflection fails. |
| | Module exports if using Java 9+ | Annotation visibility across modules. |
| | Framework docs matched | JPA, Spring, etc. require exact annotations. |
| **Processing** | `META-INF/services` entry exists | Processor is discovered. |
| | `@SupportedAnnotationTypes` includes all needed | Processor sees all annotations. |
| | Tests with processor enabled | Bugs surface early. |
| **Tooling** | IDE inspections enabled | Catch most issues early. |
| | Static analysis rules added | Continuous quality gate. |

---

## Final Words

Annotations in Java are *meta‑data*, not regular code.  
Treat them like contracts that both the *producer* (annotation author) and the *consumer* (framework or application) must honor.

By

1. **Specifying meta‑annotations** (`@Retention`, `@Target`, `@Documented`, `@Inherited`, `@Repeatable`),
2. **Using correct defaults** and **compile‑time constants**,
3. **Avoiding import confusion**, and
4. **Leveraging IDE & static‑analysis tooling**,

you can prevent almost every subtle bug that would otherwise surface only at runtime, in tests, or in production.

Happy annotating!
